Introduction
If you have ever built a trading system based on historical volatility, you know that feeling of disappointment. The system performs perfectly in the backtester: the Sharpe ratio is high and drawdowns are minimal. You then run it live, and two or three months later, you see the equity curve start to drop just as the market suddenly becomes highly volatile. Classical volatility models failed.
The problem is not with the parameters or overfitting; it runs deeper — it lies in the fundamental assumption underlying GARCH, Black-Scholes, and most classical models: that volatility changes smoothly and randomly, like a standard Brownian motion.
The real market does not behave that way.
In 2014, Gatheral, Jaisson, and Rosenbaum published
a paper
that revolutionized our understanding of the nature of volatility. They found that the volatility of financial markets is described by fractional Brownian motion with a Hurst exponent H ≈ 0.10. This number is key: it means that volatility is much rougher and more unpredictable than previously thought. Its sample path is nowhere differentiable. That is precisely why any model that assumes volatility is smooth is bound to fail.
This article explains how to turn this mathematical discovery into a working MQL5 indicator. Not a theoretical overview, but a practical tool: a moving-window Hurst exponent estimator that determines the market regime in real time and signals regime changes.
This article systematically examines:
why classical volatility models are fundamentally flawed;
the theory of fractional Brownian motion and the Hurst exponent;
rescaled range analysis (R/S analysis) — mathematics and implementation;
the complete code for the RoughVolatility_H indicator in MQL5;
signal interpretation and trading applications;
a comparison with classical volatility estimators;
practical diagnosis of the market regime.
Why Classical Volatility Models Are Fundamentally Flawed
The classic Black-Scholes model assumes that an asset's price follows geometric Brownian motion with constant volatility. This is mathematically elegant but empirically disastrously wrong. Volatility is not constant — every trader knows that. That is why GARCH was developed.
GARCH and its variants (EGARCH, GJR-GARCH, FIGARCH) allow volatility to change over time. However, they are based on another key assumption: volatility is an integer-order integrated process, meaning it is either stationary (I(0)) or non-stationary (I(1)). Both classes correspond to a Hurst exponent of H = 0.5 or H = 1.0.
Gatheral, Jaisson, and Rosenbaum (2014) conducted an empirical analysis of realized volatility data across several markets: stocks, indices, commodities, and forex. They estimated the Hurst exponent for the logarithm of realized volatility and obtained values of H ranging from 0.05 to 0.20. The average value is about 0.10. This is an order of magnitude lower than all classical models assume.
What does H = 0.10 mean in practice? Volatility movements reverse quickly; volatility spikes are sharp and abrupt — not smooth — and volatility bursts are not anomalies but a systemic characteristic. A model that does not take this into account systematically underestimates risk during calm periods and overestimates resilience during periods of turmoil.
This is precisely what explains why strategies typically fail during market crises: the GARCH model produces conditional volatility that changes too slowly to respond to a sharp spike in actual volatility.
Fractional Brownian Motion and the Hurst Exponent
A standard Brownian motion W(t) has independent increments: knowledge of the past provides no information about the future. Fractional Brownian motion (fBm) is a generalization introduced by Kolmogorov and popularized by Mandelbrot and van Ness (1968) that allows for long-term memory.
Fractional Brownian motion B
H
(t) is parameterized by a single number H ∈ (0, 1) — the Hurst exponent:
H = 0.5 — standard Brownian motion, independent increments, no memory;
H > 0.5 — a persistent process: positive increments are more likely to follow positive ones (a trending market);
H < 0.5 — an anti-persistent process: the increments are negatively correlated across all scales (a mean-reverting market);
H ≈ 0.10 — rough volatility in Gatheral's framework: an ultra-rough process that is close to being nowhere differentiable.
A key mathematical property — the scaling law for the variance of the increments of fBm:
