It is precisely this scaling law that forms the basis of R/S analysis: we observe how the rescaled range of a time series scales as the length of the subsample increases, and we estimate the scaling exponent.
Rescaled Range Analysis — R/S Analysis
R/S analysis (rescaled range analysis) was proposed by Hurst in 1951 while studying the Nile floods and was formalized by Mandelbrot and Wallis (1969). This is the most robust nonparametric estimator of the Hurst exponent.
Let r₁, r₂, ..., rₙ be a time series of log returns. For a subsample of length τ, the algorithm consists of four steps. Calculate the mean μ = (1/τ) Σ r_i. Construct the deviation profile Y_k = Σ (r_i − μ). Range: R(τ) = max(Y) − min(Y). Standard deviation: S(τ) = std(r_i). Rescaled range: R/S(τ) = R(τ) / S(τ).
According to the fBm scaling law: E[R/S(τ)] ~ c · τ
H
. In logarithmic space: log(R/S) ~ H · log(τ) + const. The Hurst exponent is the slope of a straight line in log-log coordinates, estimated using the ordinary least squares (OLS) method across several scales τ.
The implementation uses logarithmically spaced scales, ranging from the minimum (InpMinScale) to half the window (InpWindow/2). This ensures uniform coverage of the scale range without giving excessive weight to small or large scales.
