The Core of the Algorithm is the CalcHurst Function
This is the indicator's main function. It performs a full R/S analysis using OLS regression in log-log space. The logic is divided into three stages: looping over scales, accumulating R/S at each scale, and performing the final regression.
//+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| R/S analysis — estimation of the Hurst exponent                            |
//|                                                                            |
//| Parameters:                                                                |
//|   ret[]  — array of log returns                                            |
//|   start  — starting index in ret[]                                         |
//|   length — length of the analysis window                                   |
//|                                                                            |
//| Returns: H ∈ [0.01, 0.99] or EMPTY_VALUE if an error occurs                |
//|                                                                            |
//| Algorithm:                                                                 |
//|   1. For each scale n (logarithmically spaced from MinScale to N/2):       |
//|      a. Divide the window into non-overlapping subsamples of length n      |
//|      b. For each one: calculate R (range) and S (standard deviation)       |
//|      c. Calculate the average log(R/S) for a given n                       |
//|   2. OLS regression: log(R/S) ~ H * log(n) → H = the slope of the line     |
//+----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
double
CalcHurst
(
const
double
&ret[],
int
start,
int
length)
  {
//--- Boundary check
if
(length < InpMinScale *
2
)
return
(
EMPTY_VALUE
);
double
logN_min =
MathLog
((
double
)InpMinScale);
double
logN_max =
MathLog
((
double
)(length >>
1
));
// log(length / 2)
if
(logN_max <= logN_min +
1e-10
)
return
(
EMPTY_VALUE
);
//--- Accumulators for linear regression in log-log space
double
sumX  =
0.0
, sumY  =
0.0
;
double
sumXY =
0.0
, sumX2 =
0.0
;
int
nPts  =
0
;
//--- Loop over logarithmically spaced scales
for
(
int
s =
0
; s < InpNumScales; s++)
     {
//--- Scale n: from InpMinScale to length/2 (log-uniform)
double
alpha = (InpNumScales >
1
) ? (
double
)s / (InpNumScales -
1
) :
0.0
;
int
n     = (
int
)
MathRound
(
MathExp
(logN_min + (logN_max - logN_min) * alpha));
      n =
MathMax
(InpMinScale,
MathMin
(length >>
1
, n));
//--- Number of non-overlapping subsamples of length n
int
numSubs = length / n;
if
(numSubs <
1
)
         continue;
double
logRS_acc =
0.0
;
int
logRS_cnt =
0
;
//--- Loop over subsamples
for
(
int
sub =
0
; sub < numSubs; sub++)
        {
int
i0 = start + sub * n;
//--- Mean return in the subsample
double
mu =
0.0
;
for
(
int
i =
0
; i < n; i++)
            mu += ret[i0 + i];
         mu /= n;
//--- Cumulative deviations, range R, and variance
double
cumDev =
0.0
;
double
R_max  = -
1e30
;
double
R_min  =
1e30
;
double
var    =
0.0
;
for
(
int
i =
0
; i < n; i++)
           {
double
d  = ret[i0 + i] - mu;
            cumDev   += d;
            var      += d * d;
if
(cumDev > R_max)
               R_max = cumDev;
if
(cumDev < R_min)
               R_min = cumDev;
           }
double
R = R_max - R_min;
// range of cumulative deviations
double
S =
MathSqrt
(var / n);
// standard deviation
//--- Accumulate log(R/S) if both values are numerically significant
if
(S >
1e-12
&& R >
1e-12
)
           {
            logRS_acc +=
MathLog
(R / S);
            logRS_cnt++;
           }
        }
if
(logRS_cnt <
1
)
         continue;
//--- Average log(R/S) for the scale n → regression point
double
logRS = logRS_acc / logRS_cnt;
double
logN  =
MathLog
((
double
)n);

      sumX  += logN;
      sumY  += logRS;
      sumXY += logN * logRS;
      sumX2 += logN * logN;
      nPts++;
     }
//--- OLS: slope in log-log space = Hurst exponent
if
(nPts <
2
)
return
(
EMPTY_VALUE
);
double
det = (
double
)nPts * sumX2 - sumX * sumX;
if
(
MathAbs
(det) <
1e-14
)
return
(
EMPTY_VALUE
);
double
H = ((
double
)nPts * sumXY - sumX * sumY) / det;
//--- Clamp to the physically permissible range
return
(
MathMax
(
0.01
,
MathMin
(
0.99
, H)));
  }
Logarithmically spaced scales.
Instead of evenly spaced values (8, 16, 24, ...), exponentially spaced values (8, 13, 22, 36, ...) are used. This ensures uniform coverage in log space and prevents small scales from being overrepresented, as they yield more observation points but less independent information.
Averaging over subsamples.
For a scale of n, the window is divided into several non-overlapping subsamples, and R/S is averaged over them. This reduces the variance of the estimate. When n is small, there are many subsamples, and the estimate is stable. When n is large, there are few subsamples — hence the importance of the InpWindow parameter: the larger it is, the more reliable the estimate at large scales.
Numerical stability.
The conditions S &gt; 1e-12 and R &gt; 1e-12 prevent division by zero when dealing with degenerate data. The condition |det| &gt; 1e-14 prevents OLS instability when the matrices are degenerate.
