Choosing a Strategy Type (Trend-Following/Countertrend)
Practical combination: ATR determines the position size and SL/TP levels, while RoughVolatility_H determines the strategy type. When H < 0.3, we use mean-reversion strategies. When H > 0.5, we use momentum strategies. When H is between 0.3 and 0.5, we reduce position size or refrain from entering a trade.
Practical Diagnosis of the Market Regime
In practice, the indicator behaves as follows under various market conditions.
A calm market (low ATR, narrow Bollinger Bands).
The Hurst exponent is usually in the range of 0.4–0.6; the line is blue. The market is in a neutral state with no distinct market regime. Exercise caution when entering positions.
A period of high volatility (news events, crises).
H drops rapidly to 0.1–0.2, and the line turns red. This is precisely where the roughness of volatility becomes apparent: spikes are sharp and involve reversals. Momentum strategies fail here; countertrend approaches come out on top.
A sustained trend (a directional movement lasting several weeks).
H rises above 0.55–0.70; the line is green. This is a rare but valuable state: trend-following systems produce the best results. Important: The green regime often precedes a sharp shift to red when a trend reverses.
A regime change as a trading signal.
A shift from the blue zone to the red zone (InpAlerts alert) often occurs before a sharp price movement. It is not the direction, but the very fact of a regime change that serves as a warning of increased risk. This is a signal to review open positions and reduce leverage.
Action Plan in Case of Deteriorating Metrics:
If H remains consistently below 0.3 for more than 50 bars: switch to countertrend strategies and reduce leverage;
H jumps erratically between zones: increase InpWindow to stabilize it, reduce the trade entry frequency;
H &gt; 0.7 across multiple time frames simultaneously: a rare occurrence; trend-following systems yield the best results, but the risk of a reversal is elevated.
Conclusion: What the Trader Gets
When a trader faces the classic problem — a strategy that works in a backtester but underperforms in the real market during a regime change — the solution lies not in tweaking parameters or using a more complex model, but in understanding the physics of the market. Rough Volatility is not just another moving-average-based indicator. This is a quantitative assessment of the market regime based on the mathematics of fractional Brownian motion.
Three practical uses available immediately after installation.
First, a strategy-type filter
. Before each session, we check the color of the line. Red zone — use countertrend tools (RSI overbought signals, return to the Bollinger Bands). Green zone — use trend-following tools (level breakouts, momentum). Blue zone — reduce position size by 50% or wait for a clear signal.
Second, risk management
. The market is particularly unpredictable in the red zone. This is a quantitative regime risk metric: reduce leverage, tighten the stop-loss, and lower the number of open positions at any given time.
Third, system diagnostics
. If a trend-following system starts to incur losses, the first question is: What is the value of H? If H &lt; 0.3, that does not mean the strategy has failed. This means that the market regime has shifted. Knowing this, you will not over-optimize your strategy; instead, you will simply wait for the market to return to a trending regime.
The indicator requires a sufficient number of bars (Window + several hundred for warm-up) to produce a stable estimate. Extreme values of H < 0.05 or H > 0.95 are most often artifacts of a small sample size rather than genuine signals.
Thus, the trader obtains a reproducible, testable metric of volatility structure — a tool for early detection of market regime changes that enables a shift from meaningless parameter fitting to strategy and risk management based on the market's physical properties.
